COPY public.essays (id, user_id, prompt, essay, overall_score, created_at) FROM stdin;
1	b5846b73-82e1-453e-971c-a52f7f3291e8	More and more people are visiting historic sites and sometimes tourists damage these ancient sites. The number of people visiting these places should be strictly limited. Do you agree or disagree?	Introduction\nBegin by paraphrasing the question and clearly stating your position. For example:\n\n"In recent years, the increasing number of tourists visiting historic sites has led to concerns about potential damage to these ancient treasures. While some argue that the number of visitors should be strictly limited to protect these sites, I believe that rather than imposing strict limits, other measures can be taken to both preserve these sites and allow people to appreciate their historical significance."\n\nBody Paragraph 1: Agree with Limiting Visitors\nYou might begin by discussing the merits of limiting visitors:\n\nPreservation of Sites: Limiting the number of visitors can help reduce wear and tear on these fragile structures. Overcrowding can lead to physical damage, as well as increase the burden on local resources.\nControlled Environment: By restricting access, it is easier to manage the environment around these sites, ensuring that the necessary conservation efforts are not overwhelmed by the sheer number of people.\nBody Paragraph 2: Disagree with Limiting Visitors\nThen, present the opposing view, which might align with your position:\n\nEducation and Awareness: Allowing more people to visit these sites fosters a greater understanding and appreciation of history. Education and awareness programs can teach visitors how to respect and protect these sites, which might be more effective than simply limiting access.\nEconomic Benefits: Historic sites often generate significant revenue through tourism, which can be used for their preservation. Limiting access could negatively impact local economies that rely on this income.\nBody Paragraph 3: Alternative Solutions\nIf you partially agree but see other ways to address the issue, you can introduce alternative solutions:\n\nTimed Entry and Advanced Booking: Instead of strictly limiting the number of visitors, implementing timed entry slots and requiring advanced booking can manage the flow of tourists more effectively.\nEnhanced Conservation Efforts: Investing in better conservation techniques, such as protective barriers, reinforced structures, and digital preservation methods (like virtual tours), can help protect these sites without restricting visitor access.\nConclusion\nSummarize your main points and restate your position:\n\n"In conclusion, while limiting the number of visitors to historic sites may help in their preservation, it is not the only solution. By educating the public, implementing timed entry, and investing in conservation efforts, we can protect these sites for future generations while still allowing people to experience and learn from them."\n\n	7.50	2024-08-16 16:00:38.551869
2	b5846b73-82e1-453e-971c-a52f7f3291e8	More and more people are visiting historic sites and sometimes tourists damage these ancient sites. The number of people visiting these places should be strictly limited. Do you agree or disagree?	Introduction\nBegin by paraphrasing the question and clearly stating your position. For example:\n\n"In recent years, the increasing number of tourists visiting historic sites has led to concerns about potential damage to these ancient treasures. While some argue that the number of visitors should be strictly limited to protect these sites, I believe that rather than imposing strict limits, other measures can be taken to both preserve these sites and allow people to appreciate their historical significance."\n\nBody Paragraph 1: Agree with Limiting Visitors\nYou might begin by discussing the merits of limiting visitors:\n\nPreservation of Sites: Limiting the number of visitors can help reduce wear and tear on these fragile structures. Overcrowding can lead to physical damage, as well as increase the burden on local resources.\nControlled Environment: By restricting access, it is easier to manage the environment around these sites, ensuring that the necessary conservation efforts are not overwhelmed by the sheer number of people.\nBody Paragraph 2: Disagree with Limiting Visitors\nThen, present the opposing view, which might align with your position:\n\nEducation and Awareness: Allowing more people to visit these sites fosters a greater understanding and appreciation of history. Education and awareness programs can teach visitors how to respect and protect these sites, which might be more effective than simply limiting access.\nEconomic Benefits: Historic sites often generate significant revenue through tourism, which can be used for their preservation. Limiting access could negatively impact local economies that rely on this income.\nBody Paragraph 3: Alternative Solutions\nIf you partially agree but see other ways to address the issue, you can introduce alternative solutions:\n\nTimed Entry and Advanced Booking: Instead of strictly limiting the number of visitors, implementing timed entry slots and requiring advanced booking can manage the flow of tourists more effectively.\nEnhanced Conservation Efforts: Investing in better conservation techniques, such as protective barriers, reinforced structures, and digital preservation methods (like virtual tours), can help protect these sites without restricting visitor access.\nConclusion\nSummarize your main points and restate your position:\n\n"In conclusion, while limiting the number of visitors to historic sites may help in their preservation, it is not the only solution. By educating the public, implementing timed entry, and investing in conservation efforts, we can protect these sites for future generations while still allowing people to experience and learn from them."\n\n	7.00	2024-08-16 16:02:50.187141
3	b5846b73-82e1-453e-971c-a52f7f3291e8	Many people believe that companies and individuals should pay to clean up the environment in proportion to the amount of pollution they have produced. Do you agree or disagree?	Individuals are argued that industries and individually people should donate money to maintain public places that they have destroyed. I completely agree that investment on the environment protection is a crucial part of human existence. In this essay, I will discuss the important of clean up public places.\n\nTo commence with, many people believe that companies and individuals should provide money on clean up their public places, the reason is that factories are dump their disposal items to the environment. That way the environment becomes contaminated because these companies are dump detrimental chemicals, whereby,  chemicals release to air or water and people would exposure to these chemicals without knowing. As a result, it will affect people's health because they may breathe or consume harmful contained water and foods. For instance, according to a study, by the University of Oxford, harmful chemicals have been discovered several chemicals on soil and it caused by cancer disease. That way, investing money for the environment protection can reduce the impact. I think they are mainly responsible for these problems.\n\nFurthermore, companies' investments are integral part of the global warming reduction plan. Because air pollution would affect the ozone layer and a long-term plan and strategies could protect these devastating problems. For example, in Dubai, people have been cleaning their own doorstep their own expense, that way it would help to the government budget. Thus, people would determine to clean surround nature and neglect pollute around because they are spend their own money and action to attain their nature beautifully.\n\nIn conclusion, most people believe that companies and individuals should provide money on clean up their nature, I strongly believe that people should donate money on clean the environment protection because if they neglect to clean up their around	5.00	2024-08-16 17:34:19.310207
